\section{Pick a Program That Crosses Every Ownership Boundary}

Audit a toy language with lexical scopes, capture by reference,
fixed-width integer addition, and mutable local variables. The
following program makes a closure over two locals. Evaluate
\texttt{a = maker(false)}, \texttt{b = maker(true)}, then
\texttt{a()}, \texttt{b()}, and \texttt{a()} in that order.
The calls return 1, 2, and 2. The two final results happen to
agree, but there is no aliasing between the two counters.

\begin{verbatim}
fn maker(flag) {
    let step = if flag then 2 else 1;
    var total = 0;
    return fn() {
        total = total + step;
        return total;
    };
}
\end{verbatim}

The audit follows name resolution in the sense of Appel's
distinction between symbols and bindings \cite{appel2004modern},
block arguments as found in MLIR \cite{lattner2021mlir}, and a
toy stack machine with a different layout from Ribbit's linked
cell stack. No production compiler or VM is assumed to have this
entire combination. At each boundary, identify the lookup key,
the entity that owns it, and the ways that a once-valid fact
stops being valid. This is more informative than asking whether
the implementation uses arrays or hash tables in the abstract.

\section{Trace One Spelling Through Three Forms of Identity}

The declaration and three uses of \texttt{total} share one
spelling ID. Each occurrence has its own source span. The
resolver records all uses against one declaration ID in the
source, but two calls of \texttt{maker} require two separate
runtime locations. Store this distinction in the interfaces:
a use resolves to a binding; closure analysis assigns that
binding a capture slot in the nested function's layout; closure
construction fills that slot with the enclosing activation's
cell. If one procedure looks up the capture by spelling ID
while another looks it up by capture slot, shadowing can
pass tests with distinct names and still fail in real programs.

\Cref{fig:visual-429} shows the point where one lexical binding
becomes multiple cells. Neither a cell nor a source span is
an appropriate input to the linker as a name. Conversely an
artifact-local name index cannot resurrect the original
activation. Reaching an entity via a chain of translations is
not evidence that the identifiers in the chain are equivalent.

