\section{Inspect the Join Before and After Simplification}

The generic \texttt{maker} can lower its initializer to a branch
in entry \(E\), with paths \(T\) and \(F\) passing 2 and 1
respectively to a join \(J(s)\). The definition of \(s\) is a
block parameter. To remove the path blocks, allow a conditional
branch with two edge occurrences \(E\to J\), passing the two
constants directly; this is legal if the constants are now
available at \(E\) and no eliminated block has effects. The
new predecessor index for \(J\) still has two entries.
Assert that the edge-argument uses now occur at \(E\), the
old handles for the deleted blocks fail validation, and the
dominance tree is recomputed for the new graph. All these
checks address separate obligations even though the edge
count into \(J\) remains two.

At closure lowering, use \(s\) to initialize a local slot
for \texttt{step}. The pure SSA value representing its
initial content is not the storage for mutable \texttt{total}.
Introduce the latter as a local with a location that can be
captured, rather than making mutation of the cell look like
an SSA definition of the same value. Installing a capture
makes initialization visible through the cell. Since
\texttt{step} never changes in this example, a later compiler
could specialize its capture mode to by-value; the baseline
keeps both by-reference, which makes the teardown contract
uniform.

