\section{Run One Harness Against Clean and Incremental Builds}

\begin{verbatim}
audit(snapshot, editor):
    clean := compile_from_scratch(snapshot)
    reused := editor.compile(snapshot)
    for build in [clean, reused]:
        assert reconstruct_bytes(build.syntax) == snapshot.bytes
        assert checked_resolution_matches_source(build)
        verify_control_edges_and_uses(build.ir)
        verify_dominance_against_slow_solver(build.ir)
        verify_stack_and_capture_layout(build.code)
        assert run_factory_script(build.code) == expected(snapshot)
    assert compare_normalized_outputs(clean, reused)

run_factory_script(code):
    vm := fresh_vm(code)
    a := vm.call("maker", false)
    b := vm.call("maker", true)
    vm.force_stack_growth()
    return [vm.call(a), vm.call(b), vm.call(a)]
\end{verbatim}

The test harness compares named source occurrences and
normalized structural results rather than allocation numbers.
For this fixture \texttt{expected} is an independent table
maintained with the test edits, not a number obtained from
the compiler being audited. In the runtime trace, after
\texttt{maker} returns the cells are closed. Growing or reusing
the stack must not change the values read through them.
Independently exercise stack growth before return to check that
an open upvalue uses an index, not a stale interior pointer.
Add a frame that returns two closures over one activation and
assert they do alias; testing only nonaliasing would accept a
VM that accidentally copies a cell for each closure.

\section{Separate an Edit's Semantic Impact From Its Coordinate Impact}

First insert a comment before the nested function. The
diagnostic projection and byte coordinates change, but
bytecode without source maps need not. A reused green subtree
does not prove the old source span remains valid, and a query
returning locations cannot simply compare the old and new
result by green shape. Next replace \texttt{else 1} with
\texttt{else 3}. The new factory-call sequence gives 3, 2,
and 6. That edit can leave name resolution untouched but
changes the false branch value, the join's inputs, and
compiled code. If the runtime supports publishing new code
while existing closures remain live, make that a separate
test: a closure over an old code object and activation is
not automatically migrated by a new compile. Here each audit
runs a fresh VM and promises no migration.

Finish by collecting retained source snapshots, arena slots
versus live IR nodes, dataflow scratch size, dirty queries
versus executed queries, and open versus closed upvalue
counts. A phase is ready to optimize when its input/output
equivalence is measurable and a slow reference model can
reject stale indexes. Profile the whole pipeline after
changing a container: saving allocations in the parser is
not progress if a new cache root keeps every previous file
revision live. The point of the audit is to identify which
contracts changed and which did not, not to mandate one
data structure across the compiler and runtime.

\section{Inject a Representation Fault Where the Audit Can Localize It}

Use fault-injection fixtures in a debug build, one at a time.
The relevant check should fail before downstream outputs
are compared; a plain output mismatch does not distinguish
a graph rewrite error from a bytecode capture error.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Injected defect & First check expected to fail \\
\hline
Use raw green ID as source position & Occurrence span after comment insertion \\
Merge parallel CFG edges by endpoint & Join argument and edge multiplicity check \\
Leave old reverse operand index & Reconstructed use-list comparison \\
Reuse a slot before closing its capture & Factory growth and activation reuse trace \\
Skip invalidation of the false literal & Incremental build after \texttt{else 3} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Also run a variant with a same-spelled local \texttt{total}
inside a new nested block. First read the outer \texttt{total},
then declare the inner one, then read it. The two reads
must have different binding IDs despite one spelling ID.
This variant distinguishes an occurrence mapping bug from
the earlier duplicate-activation fixture, where the source
binding must remain the same. Record which contract each
fixture exercises before optimizing the data structure that
implements it; otherwise a later refactor can remove the
test case that was the only one to distinguish the two.
